<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="orthogonal-functions-complete">
    <title>Orthogonal Sets of Functions (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We've seen some familiar properties of sine and cosine. There is a less familiar property 
                        that make these functions very useful in describing function behavior, which is what we will
                        explore today.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
            <title>Inner Products</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        An <term>inner product</term> is
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        an operation that combines two <q>vectors</q> to produce a <q>scalar</q> (a number) which satisfies 
                        particular properties. We won't worry about writing down those properties in detail, but we'll see 
                        some examples to give the idea.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task workspace="3in">
                <title>Dot Product</title>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                        For <m>(x_1,y_1), (x_2,y_2) \in \R^2</m>, 
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            the <term>dot product</term> of <m>(x_1,y_1), (x_2,y_2)</m> is an inner product, defined as 
                            <md>
                                (x_1,y_1) \cdot (x_2,y_2) = x_1  x_2 +  y_1 y _2
                            </md>.

                            Labeling <m>\vvec_1 =(x_1,y_1), \vvec_2 = (x_2,y_2)</m>, etc., we can see that this definition of dot
                            product satisfies
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\vvec_1 \cdot \vvec_2 = \vvec_2 \cdot \vvec_1</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>(\vvec_1 + \vvec_2)\cdot \vvec_3 = \vvec_1 \cdot \vvec_3 + \vvec_2 \cdot \vvec_3</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>c\vvec_1 \cdot \vvec_2 = c (\vvec_1 \cdot \vvec_2)</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\vvec_1 \cdot \vvec_1 \geq 0 </m>, and <m>\vvec_1 \cdot \vvec_1 = 0 </m> exactly
                                        when <m>\vvec_1 = (0,0)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>

                <task workspace="3in">
                <title>Real Functions</title>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                        For <m>f,g:\R \to \R</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, 
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            we can define an inner product of <m>f,g</m> as 
                            <md>
                                \langle f , g \rangle = \int_a^b f(x) g(x) \quad dx
                            </md>.
                            We can verify that this inner product satisfies
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\langle f , g \rangle = \langle g , f \rangle</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\langle f + h , g \rangle = \langle f , g \rangle + \langle h, g \rangle</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\langle cf , g \rangle = c\langle f , g \rangle</m>, <m>c \in \R</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\langle f , f \rangle \geq 0</m>, and <m>\langle f , f \rangle = 0</m> exactly
                                        when <m>f(x)=0</m> (almost) everywhere on <m>[a,b]</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise>
                <title>Orthogonal Functions</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                    Two non-zero <q>vectors</q> are <term>orthogonal</term> with respect to an inner product if
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        their inner product is zero.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task workspace="2in">
                <title>Dot Product</title>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                    
                            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Orthogonality with respect to the dot product tells us when two vectors are perpendicular 
                            geometrically. As an example, sketch the vectors <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(0,1)</m> in <m>\R^2</m>
                            and compute their dot product.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>

                <task workspace="2in">
                <title>Real Functions</title>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Non-zero real functions <m>f,g</m> are orthogonal on <m>[a,b]</m> if 
                            <md>
                                \int_a^b f(x) g(x) \quad dx = 0
                            </md>.
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                
                
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Orthogonal Sets</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        A set of non-zero real functions <m>\{f_i\}</m> is an <term>orthogonal set of functions</term> on
                        <m>[a,b]</m> if
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>f_i,f_j</m> are orthogonal whenever <m>i \neq j</m>. That is 
                        <md>
                            \int_a^b f_i(x) f_j(x) \quad dx = 
                            \begin{cases}
                            0 \amp \text{if } i \neq j \\
                            c_i \gt 0 \amp \text{if } i =j
                            \end{cases}
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
        </page>

        <page>
            <exercise workspace="8in">
            <title>Important Examples of Orthogonal Sets</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The following are examples of orthogonal sets of functions discussed in class:
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\{\cos\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \}_{n=0}^\infty</m> on <m>[-L,L]</m>, since
                                <md>
                                    \int_{-L}^L \cos\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \cos\left( \frac{m\pi}{L} x \right) \quad dx =
                                    \begin{cases}
                                    0 \amp \text{if } n \neq m \\
                                    2L \amp \text{if } i =j =0 \\
                                    L \amp \text{if } i = j \neq 0
                                    \end{cases}
                                </md>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\{\cos\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \}_{n=0}^\infty</m> on <m>[0,L]</m>, since
                                <md>
                                    \int_{0}^L \cos\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \cos\left( \frac{m\pi}{L} x \right) \quad dx =
                                    \begin{cases}
                                    0 \amp \text{if } n \neq m \\
                                    L \amp \text{if } i =j =0 \\
                                    L/2 \amp \text{if } i = j \neq 0
                                    \end{cases}
                                </md>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\{\sin\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \}_{n=1}^\infty</m> on <m>[-L,L]</m>, since
                                <md>
                                    \int_{-L}^L \sin\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \sin\left( \frac{m\pi}{L} x \right) \quad dx =
                                    \begin{cases}
                                    0 \amp \text{if } n \neq m \\
                                    L \amp \text{if } i =j 
                                    \end{cases}
                                </md>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\{\sin\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \}_{n=1}^\infty</m> on <m>[0,L]</m>, since
                                <md>
                                    \int_{0}^L \sin\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \sin\left( \frac{m\pi}{L} x \right) \quad dx =
                                    \begin{cases}
                                    0 \amp \text{if } n \neq m \\
                                    L/2 \amp \text{if } i =j 
                                    \end{cases}
                                </md>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\{\sin\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \}_{n=1}^\infty \cup \{\cos\left( \frac{n\pi}{L} x \right) \}_{n=0}^\infty</m> on <m>[-L,L]</m>,
                                but is <em>not</em> an orthogonal set on <m>[0,L]</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
                
            </exercise>
        </page>

         <page>
            <exercise workspace="4in">
                <title>Superposition</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>\psi_1, \psi_2</m> are valid state functions, so is
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>\psi = c_1 \psi_1 + c_2 \psi_2</m> (a <term>linear combination</term>).
                        We also technically need to <term>normalize</term> <m>\psi</m> for it to be valid, which we
                        will discuss more shortly.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                      If <m>\psi_1, \psi_2</m> are also orthogonal on <m>[-L,L]</m>, we have a method for determining the coefficients which would 
                      give us <m>\psi</m>. We'll use this idea on an infinite set of orthogonal functions when we discuss Fourier series at 
                      the end of the semester.
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
        </page>

       
    </worksheet>
